.TH DWIM 1 2026-09-19 "dwim 0.0.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dwim \- a coding agent
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dwim
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ PROMPT ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dwim
is a coding agent that runs in your terminal.
It reads and edits files, runs commands, and works through a task with you in
an interactive shell.
.PP
With no arguments,
.B dwim
starts an interactive session in the current directory.
If
.I PROMPT
is given,
.B dwim
answers it without the shell and exits: the reply goes to standard output and
nothing else does, while progress, the model's thinking, the commands it runs,
and their output go to standard error.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-model " model"
Model to run (default:
.BR bonsai\-2\-27b ).
.TP
.BI \-\-device " device"
Device to run the model on:
.B gpu
(default), or
.BR cpu .
.TP
.BI \-\-context " tokens"
Maximum conversation length in tokens (default: 32768).
Cannot exceed the model's supported context length.
Larger values reserve more memory on the selected device at startup.
The conversation is compacted before it outgrows the window: when a
message or a command's output would take it past four fifths, the agent
writes itself a note of the task, what it found, what it did and what is
next, starts over from the system prompt, and reads back the note, your
earlier messages, and the most recent exchanges without their thoughts.
A reply that runs out of room is stopped short and goes on after a
compaction.
.TP
.B \-\-stats
When answering a prompt given on the command line, print where the time
went once the reply ends: the time loading the model; the tokens read as
prompts, generated while thinking, and generated as the reply, with the
time and rate of each; and the tools run.
.TP
.B \-\-thinking
Show the model's thinking in the shell from the start, as
.B Ctrl\-O
does.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display usage information.
.SH INTERACTIVE SHELL
The shell reads one message at a time.
Anything you type is sent to the agent.
Lines starting with a slash are commands:
.TP
.B /model
List the models
.B dwim
knows, where their weights are kept and how much of each has been fetched,
marking the one running.
.TP
.B /stats
Show where the conversation's time has gone, as
.B \-\-stats
does.
.TP
.B /compact
Compact the conversation now, as happens on its own when it nears the
end of the context window.
.PP
The model thinks before it replies, and the shell hides the thought unless
.B Ctrl\-O
shows it: pressed while the model thinks, it prints the thought so far and
what follows, and pressed again it hides the rest.
.B Ctrl\-C
or
.B Esc
interrupts the agent while it works.
.B Ctrl\-C
or
.B Ctrl\-D
on an empty line exits
.BR dwim ,
and
.B Ctrl\-C
on a line with text clears it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B AGENTS.md
If present in the working directory, its contents are given to the agent as
project instructions.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/dwim/states/
The model's state after the system prompt, saved when a prompt is first
read and taken up by later runs with the same model and prompt in place of
reading it again. The prompt changes only with the project's
.BR AGENTS.md ,
since where the agent is, the files there, the branch and the date go with
the first message instead, and the newest four states are kept. On macOS the
directory is
.I ~/Library/Caches/dwim/states/
instead.
.TP
.I $TMPDIR/dwim-<pid>-<n>/
Where the whole output of a command that printed more than the model is
shown is kept, readable by the user alone, as a file per stream named by the
command's number, such as
.IR 3.stdout .
The result the model sees names the file. The directory is removed when
.B dwim
exits, and any left by an earlier
.B dwim
that is no longer running are removed by the next one to start.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR claude (1),
.BR codex (1),
.BR opencode (1)
